WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.150
Today, I'm going
to talk about how

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.760
to make Shiny apps faster
with caching using features

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.110
from the new version
of Shiny version 1.6.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.510
Here's an app that shows
average weather patterns

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.880
over the course of a
year, so let's take a look

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.115
at the weather where
I live in Minneapolis.

00:00:20.115 --> 00:00:22.190
We'll load the data.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.120
And the weather here
is pretty typical for

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.370
the continental United States.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.670
It's hot in the summer,
cold in the winter,

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:34.190
and it rains more in the
summer, and less in the winter.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.650
I have relatives that live in
the Bay Area in California.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.570
And sometimes, I compare
my weather to theirs,

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.250
and I feel a little sad.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.110
But then I compare
real estate prices,

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.800
and I feel a lot better.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.600
So here's the weather for
Redwood City, California.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.330
In the summer, it's warm.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.610
In the winter, it's
cooler, but it doesn't ever

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.440
get really cold.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.300
And in the winter,
it rains a lot.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.670
And in the summer, it
rains almost not at all.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.023
Now with this app,
there's a button

00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:10.190
that you can use to switch
between the previous city

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:11.190
and the current one.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:16.280
So let's switch to Minneapolis
by clicking on there, and back

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.955
to Redwood City by
clicking on it again.

00:01:19.955 --> 00:01:21.330
But you notice
that every time we

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.400
do that, it has to rego
out and refetch the data.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.470
And then redraw the plots,
and that takes time.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.370
And so this experience of
switching back and forth

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.590
isn't really a great one.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.760
Now how can we make it better?

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.540
Well, the answer
is with caching.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.760
Here's some simplified
code from the app.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.760
We have the weather data,
which is a reactive expression.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.700
And that calls a function
called fetchData,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.890
and you give that the city that
you want to fetch the data for.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.140
That goes out to the internet.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.078
It grabs the data,
and then it returns.

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:52.870
This takes a while.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.500
This is the slowest
part of the application.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.720
In Shiny 1.6, we've added a
function called bindCache.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.620
The way it works is you pass
your reactive expression

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.870
to the bindCache
function, and then you

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.060
tell it what to use
as the cache key.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.665
In this case, it's
input dollar city.

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.540
So the first time you
have a particular value

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.160
of input dollar city, it will
run the reactive expression,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.380
and store the
value in the cache.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.458
And then if you ever have
that same value again,

00:02:23.458 --> 00:02:25.750
instead of having to recompute
the reactive expression,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.640
it can just fetch the old
value out of the cache.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.390
And in this case, that
means we don't know

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.700
we don't have to go to the
internet to fetch the data.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.150
The other part of our app
that takes significant time

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.790
is the plot rendering
with renderPlot.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.940
And as it turns out, you can
use bindCache with a renderPlot

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.180
as well.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.150
So that the plots for a
particular city instead

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.610
of having to be
recomputed every time,

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.770
they can be simply
fetched from the cache.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.730
And this can really
speed things up.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:58.090
OK, let's look at app again,
but this time with caching.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.290
So all we did was add bindCache
to the reactive expression

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.230
and to the renderPlots,
and that's it.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.450
Now when we look
at these cities,

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.790
and switch back and
forth between them,

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.088
it's pretty much instantaneous.

00:03:11.088 --> 00:03:12.880
Because instead of
having to fetch the data

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.100
and recompute the
plots, it just grabs

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.480
the plots and the data
right out of the cache,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.090
which is very, very fast.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:23.290
So we've seen bindCache use with
reactive and with renderPlot.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.660
And I should mention
that before Shiny 1.6,

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.420
it was possible to use
caching with plots by using

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.240
the renderCachedPlot function.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.340
But that's now equivalent
to using a renderPlot

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.590
and bindCache.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.870
But it doesn't stop
there, you can also

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.880
use bindCache with other render
functions like renderText.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.190
And it will even work
with render functions that

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.880
are not in Shiny itself
like renderPlotly

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.530
from the plotly package.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.040
All you have to do is add
bindCache to the end of it.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.320
If you've written one of
these render functions,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.550
in most cases, it will
just work with bindCache.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.100
But if not, typically the
changes that you need to make

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.890
are quite small just
a few lines of code.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.680
There are a few details you
might be wondering about.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.780
So first is the
size of the cache.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.410
The default size is 200
megabytes in memory.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.880
Now that size can be changed.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.980
And instead of using
it memory, the cache

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.140
can also be stored on disk.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:21.620
Next, the scope.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.960
The cache is shared across all
user sessions of an application

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.110
by default. And that means
that if one person fetches

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.930
a particular data set or
views a particular plot,

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:35.000
then other users can
benefit from that.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.300
But if that's a problem for
your particular use case,

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.590
then the cache can be scoped
to just one session instead.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:43.840
Next, the lifetime.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.400
A memory cache is discarded when
the app exits or it restarts.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.120
But a disk based cache can
persist across app restarts,

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.850
and can even be shared across
multiple simultaneous R

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.040
processes serving
up the same app

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.220
as you might do with
Connect or Shiny Server Pro.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.170
Thanks for listening,
and I hope that you

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:05.200
find bindCache useful for
speeding up and scaling up

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.770
your Shiny applications.

